<section xml:id="sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules">
  <title>Numerical integration rules</title>

  <introduction><p>Numerical integration approximates</p>

  <p><me>I=\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</me></p>

  <p>by replacing the graph with simple shapes.</p>

  <p>Let <m>a&lt;b</m> and let <m>n</m> be a positive integer. Set</p>

  <p><me>\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n},
\qquad
x_j=a+j\Delta x,
\qquad
j=0,1,\ldots,n.</me></p>

  <p>The midpoint of the <m>j</m>-th subinterval <m>[x_{j-1},x_j]</m> is</p>

  <p><me>m_j=\frac{x_{j-1}+x_j}{2}.</me></p>

  </introduction><subsection xml:id="appsubsec-numerical-rules-table">
    <title>Rectangle, midpoint, trapezoidal, and Simpson rules</title>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-0">
    <title>Rectangle, midpoint, trapezoidal, and Simpson rules</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="22%"/><col width="56%"/><col width="22%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>rule</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>formula</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>geometry</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Left rectangle rule</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
L_n=\Delta x\sum_{j=0}^{n-1} f(x_j)</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Left endpoint rectangles</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Right rectangle rule</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
R_n=\Delta x\sum_{j=1}^{n} f(x_j)</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Right endpoint rectangles</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Midpoint rule</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
M_n=\Delta x\sum_{j=1}^{n} f(m_j)</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Midpoint rectangles</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Trapezoidal rule</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle\begin{gathered}T_n=\frac{\Delta x}{2}\bigl[f(x_0)+f(x_n)\\{}+2\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}f(x_j)\bigr]\end{gathered}</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Trapezoids</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Simpson’s rule</p></cell><cell><p>For even <m>n</m>, <m>\displaystyle\begin{gathered}S_n=\frac{\Delta x}{3}\bigl[f(x_0)+f(x_n)\\{}+4\sum_{\substack{1\le j\le n-1\\j\text{ odd}}}f(x_j)\\{}+2\sum_{\substack{2\le j\le n-2\\j\text{ even}}}f(x_j)\bigr].\end{gathered}</m></p></cell><cell><p>Parabolic arcs</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
  </table>

  <note xml:id="note-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-9">
    <title>Simpson’s rule requires even <m>n</m></title>
      <p>The subintervals are paired, because each pair is approximated by a parabola.</p>
  </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-numerical-error-bounds-table">
    <title>Error bounds</title>

  <p>The following bounds assume <m>f</m> has the required derivatives on <m>[a,b]</m>.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-1">
    <title>Error bounds</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="33.3%"/><col width="33.3%"/><col width="33.4%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>rule</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>hypothesis</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>error bound</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Left or right rectangle</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle |f'(x)|\le K</m> on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
|I-L_n|\le \frac{K(b-a)^2}{2n},
\qquad
|I-R_n|\le \frac{K(b-a)^2}{2n}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Midpoint</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle |f''(x)|\le K</m> on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
|I-M_n|\le \frac{K(b-a)^3}{24n^2}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Trapezoidal</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle |f''(x)|\le K</m> on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
|I-T_n|\le \frac{K(b-a)^3}{12n^2}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Simpson</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle |f^{(4)}(x)|\le K</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, and <m>n</m> is even</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
|I-S_n|\le \frac{K(b-a)^5}{180n^4}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>These are worst-case guarantees.  The actual error may be much smaller.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-monotonicity-concavity-numerical-checks">
    <title>Monotonicity and concavity checks</title>

  <p>Before using a numerical value, a quick shape check can tell whether the approximation
should overestimate or underestimate.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-2">
    <title>Monotonicity and concavity checks</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="50.0%"/><col width="50.0%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>shape condition</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>consequence</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>f</m> increasing on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle L_n\le \int_a^b f(x)\,dx\le R_n</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>f</m> decreasing on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle R_n\le \int_a^b f(x)\,dx\le L_n</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p><m>f</m> concave up on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>The trapezoidal rule overestimates.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><m>f</m> concave down on <m>[a,b]</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>The trapezoidal rule underestimates.</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>For the midpoint rule, the concavity effect is opposite:</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-3">
    <title>Monotonicity and concavity checks</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="50.0%"/><col width="50.0%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>condition</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>midpoint rule behavior</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>concave up</p></cell>
        <cell><p>midpoint rule underestimates</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>concave down</p></cell>
        <cell><p>midpoint rule overestimates</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-choosing-numerical-rule">
    <title>Choosing a numerical rule</title>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-4">
    <title>Choosing a numerical rule</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="50%"/><col width="50%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>situation</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>reasonable choice</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Only left-endpoint data are available</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Left rectangle rule</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Only right-endpoint data are available</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Right rectangle rule</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Midpoint data are available or easy to compute</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Midpoint rule</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Endpoint data are available and graph is nearly linear on small intervals</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Trapezoidal rule</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Function values are easy to compute and high accuracy is desired</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Simpson’s rule</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>A guaranteed error tolerance is required</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Use a rule with an error bound and choose <m>n</m> from the bound.</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <note xml:id="note-sec-f-5-numerical-integration-rules-10">
    <title>Final numerical habit</title>
      <p>A decimal approximation should come with a reason to trust it:</p>
        <p><me>\text{shape check, error bound, or convergence evidence.}</me></p>
      <p>Best of all, use more than one.</p>
  </note>
  <p>Optional extensions give Richardson and Romberg extrapolation in <xref ref="sec-additional-richardson-romberg"/> and a complete adaptive Simpson implementation in <xref ref="sec-additional-adaptive-quadrature"/>. Their difference-based indicators are error estimates; certified bounds require additional hypotheses and error analysis.</p>
</subsection>

</section>
